From 274554f9bb3795765b0228cfac8c7f587c7c8d4c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 01:26:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 158/206] analyze_snapshot: the self_pp shortfall is this
 file's fixed 3-word pool skip

The F68 comment block asserted the 2 missing self_pp slots were a gen_snapshot
codegen difference in parseNumber and that "nothing in this file is
implicated". A falsification pass measured that and it is false: our prog .so
does contain the pool-1332 load in both offenders, same instruction word
0xfd54db60 (ldr d0,[x27,#10672]), at the same position in the reference stream
as theirs. The real mechanism is the DecodePoolReference branch of
ComputeShorebirdCodeFacts charging the pool form a fixed three words
(ahead = 3 - used; i += 1 + ahead), which swallows a genuine reference when our
codegen packs the site tighter than theirs -- refs landing within 2 words of
the previous ref are prog OURS 2, prog THEIRS 0, hello both 0.

Rewrites the attribution, records that this reading has now been
mis-attributed twice, keeps every measured number, retracts the "ours emits
1332 elsewhere" evidence (pool index labels are per-fixture), keeps the caveat
that their analyzer may carry the same skip and never be triggered by their own
layout, and adds a KNOWN-SUSPECT cross-reference at the skip itself.

Comment only. No code change, no rebuild.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc b/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
index e3f5a1029ea..c269195d1f1 100644
--- a/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
+++ b/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
@@ -1092,10 +1092,20 @@ static constexpr intptr_t kNoSelector = -1;
 // ⚠️ Two prog functions still come up one slot short -- index_in_entries 1580
 // and 1633, ours 14 slots against their 15, both missing pool index 1332 -- so
 // the undeduped self_pp is ordered-equal on 4594 of 4596 functions. Those two
-// slots were first written up as "our WALK differs from theirs at two sites".
-// THAT ATTRIBUTION WAS WRONG and is retracted here (F68, 2026-09-15).
